    question: "How do you test input validation for security?",
    slug: "how-do-you-test-input-validation-for-security",
    shortDescription: "Interview question on how do you test input validation for security with a practical Quality Assurance perspective.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would approach test input validation for security as a risk-based QA problem. Security testing begins with trust boundaries, identities, permissions, sensitive data, and the threat model. I test both allowed and denied behavior and verify that controls are enforced server-side. I consider authentication, authorization, session management, input validation, secrets, data exposure, rate limiting, and secure configuration. Specialized penetration testing may be required, but QA can continuously add security regression checks and verify fixes in authorized environments. I would document assumptions, use representative data, verify the expected result independently, and preserve evidence for any important failure.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you can connect testing practice to risk and release confidence. A strong answer explains what evidence you would collect and why that evidence is sufficient for the scenario.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. I would approach test input validation for security as a risk-based QA problem.\n2. Security testing begins with trust boundaries, identities, permissions, sensitive data, and the threat model.\n3. I test both allowed and denied behavior and verify that controls are enforced server-side.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nGive the normal path first, then mention the point where the operation can fail or become unsafe.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, an engineer can apply the control to an actual request or resource boundary and verify that an unauthorized action is rejected. I would approach test input validation for security as a risk-based QA problem.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn quality engineering, the concept matters when deciding what evidence is needed before release. Test scope should reflect risk, change impact, customer usage, and the cost of missing a defect.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
