    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would approach test mobile app interruptions as a risk-based QA problem. Mobile quality depends on platform lifecycle, device fragmentation, network variability, permissions, and hardware behavior. I prioritize supported device and OS combinations using real usage and business risk. I test interruptions, backgrounding, orientation, connectivity changes, notifications, storage, installation, upgrades, accessibility, and performance. Real devices are important for hardware- and OS-specific behavior, while emulators and automation help provide breadth and repeatability. I would document assumptions, use representative data, verify the expected result independently, and preserve evidence for any important failure.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you can connect testing practice to risk and release confidence. A strong answer explains what evidence you would collect and why that evidence is sufficient for the scenario.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. I would approach test mobile app interruptions as a risk-based QA problem.\n2. Mobile quality depends on platform lifecycle, device fragmentation, network variability, permissions, and hardware behavior.\n3. I prioritize supported device and OS combinations using real usage and business risk.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nGive the normal path first, then mention the point where the operation can fail or become unsafe.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. I would approach test mobile app interruptions as a risk-based QA problem.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn quality engineering, the concept matters when deciding what evidence is needed before release. Test scope should reflect risk, change impact, customer usage, and the cost of missing a defect.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
