    question: "What is the difference between scalability and elasticity?",
    slug: "scalability-vs-elasticity-cloud",
    shortDescription: "Explain the two terms.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nScalability is the ability to handle increased load by adding resources (vertical/horizontal). Elasticity is the ability to automatically scale resources up and down based on demand. Elasticity is a key advantage of cloud computing.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between scalability and elasticity and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Scalability is the ability to handle increased load by adding resources (vertical/horizontal).\n2. Elasticity is the ability to automatically scale resources up and down based on demand.\n3. Elasticity is a key advantage of cloud computing.\n4. Separate the service’s responsibility from the responsibilities of adjacent services, then state the main availability, IAM, networking, or cost consideration when relevant..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an AWS-hosted service, a request or workload can pass through the AWS component described by the answer, and the team can verify the resulting routing, storage, or compute behavior. Scalability is the ability to handle increased load by adding resources (vertical/horizontal).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an AWS-hosted service, this affects how traffic, compute, storage, identity, or monitoring behaves. Scalability is the ability to handle increased load by adding resources (vertical/horizontal).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
