    slug: "aws-overview",
    shortDescription: "List core services.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAWS (Amazon Web Services) is a comprehensive cloud platform. Core services: Compute (EC2, Lambda), Storage (S3, EBS), Databases (RDS, DynamoDB), Networking (VPC, Route53), Security (IAM, KMS), and Management (CloudWatch, CloudFormation).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question is useful because list core services. The interviewer can then probe the mechanism, limitation, or practical consequence behind that answer.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. AWS (Amazon Web Services) is a comprehensive cloud platform.\n2. Core services: Compute (EC2, Lambda), Storage (S3, EBS), Databases (RDS, DynamoDB), Networking (VPC, Route53), Security (IAM, KMS), and Management (CloudWatch, CloudFormation).\n3. Separate the service’s responsibility from the responsibilities of adjacent services, then state the main availability, IAM, networking, or cost consideration when relevant..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an AWS-hosted service, a request or workload can pass through the AWS component described by the answer, and the team can verify the resulting routing, storage, or compute behavior. AWS (Amazon Web Services) is a comprehensive cloud platform.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an AWS-hosted service, this affects how traffic, compute, storage, identity, or monitoring behaves. AWS (Amazon Web Services) is a comprehensive cloud platform.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing services that operate at different layers.\n- Omitting the request or data flow between services.\n- Ignoring IAM, networking, availability, or cost when those constraints affect the answer.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nSeparate the service’s responsibility from the surrounding architecture. Mention the key boundary or limitation instead of describing AWS as a collection of interchangeable services.",
