    slug: "vertical-vs-horizontal-scaling",
    shortDescription: "Explain scaling strategies.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nVertical scaling (scale up) increases the size (CPU, RAM) of an existing instance (e.g., EC2 instance type change). Horizontal scaling (scale out) adds more instances (e.g., using Auto Scaling groups). Horizontal scaling is preferred for elasticity and fault tolerance.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between vertical and horizontal scaling in aws and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Vertical scaling (scale up) increases the size (CPU, RAM) of an existing instance (e.g., EC2 instance type change).\n2. Horizontal scaling (scale out) adds more instances (e.g., using Auto Scaling groups).\n3. Horizontal scaling is preferred for elasticity and fault tolerance.\n4. Separate the service’s responsibility from the responsibilities of adjacent services, then state the main availability, IAM, networking, or cost consideration when relevant..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an AWS-hosted service, a request or workload can pass through the AWS component described by the answer, and the team can verify the resulting routing, storage, or compute behavior. Vertical scaling (scale up) increases the size (CPU, RAM) of an existing instance (e.g., EC2 instance type change).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an AWS-hosted service, this affects how traffic, compute, storage, identity, or monitoring behaves. Vertical scaling (scale up) increases the size (CPU, RAM) of an existing instance (e.g., EC2 instance type change).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
