    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nUsers (individual entities), Groups (collections of users), Roles (assumed by users or services to grant permissions), and Policies (JSON documents that define permissions).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Users (individual entities), Groups (collections of users), Roles (assumed by users or services to grant permissions), and Policies (JSON documents that define permissions).\n2. Separate the service’s responsibility from the responsibilities of adjacent services, then state the main availability, IAM, networking, or cost consideration when relevant..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nName the common items first and give a short purpose for each. Spend most of the time on the distinction most likely to matter in the role.\n\n**Example :**\nA production service can use an IAM role for its compute workload so the application obtains temporary permissions rather than embedding long-lived access keys in configuration.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an AWS workload, what are the key components of iam affects a specific part of the request, compute, storage, identity, network, or operations path. The design should be evaluated against availability, security, scaling, and cost requirements.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
