    question: "What is the difference between an IAM user and an IAM role?",
    slug: "user-vs-role",
    shortDescription: "Explain the distinction.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAn IAM user is a specific identity with permanent credentials. An IAM role is a temporary identity that can be assumed by users, applications, or services. Roles are used to grant permissions without creating long-term credentials.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between an iam user and an iam role and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. An IAM user is a specific identity with permanent credentials.\n2. An IAM role is a temporary identity that can be assumed by users, applications, or services.\n3. Roles are used to grant permissions without creating long-term credentials.\n4. Separate the service’s responsibility from the responsibilities of adjacent services, then state the main availability, IAM, networking, or cost consideration when relevant..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nA production service can use an IAM role for its compute workload so the application obtains temporary permissions rather than embedding long-lived access keys in configuration.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an AWS-hosted service, this affects how traffic, compute, storage, identity, or monitoring behaves. An IAM user is a specific identity with permanent credentials.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
