    slug: "iam-policy-structure",
    shortDescription: "Explain the JSON format.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAn IAM policy is a JSON document that defines permissions. Structure: `Version`, `Statement` (list), each statement has `Effect` (Allow/Deny), `Action` (API calls), `Resource` (ARNs), and optional `Condition`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain an IAM policy and what is the structure of a policy document accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. An IAM policy is a JSON document that defines permissions.\n2. Structure: `Version`, `Statement` (list), each statement has `Effect` (Allow/Deny), `Action` (API calls), `Resource` (ARNs), and optional `Condition`.\n3. Separate the service’s responsibility from the responsibilities of adjacent services, then state the main availability, IAM, networking, or cost consideration when relevant..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nA production service can use an IAM role for its compute workload so the application obtains temporary permissions rather than embedding long-lived access keys in configuration.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an AWS-hosted service, this affects how traffic, compute, storage, identity, or monitoring behaves. An IAM policy is a JSON document that defines permissions.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing services that operate at different layers.\n- Omitting the request or data flow between services.\n- Ignoring IAM, networking, availability, or cost when those constraints affect the answer.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nSeparate the service’s responsibility from the surrounding architecture. Mention the key boundary or limitation instead of describing AWS as a collection of interchangeable services.",
