    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nStopping an instance powers it down; EBS volumes persist. You can start it again later. Terminating deletes the instance and associated EBS volumes (unless configured otherwise).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between stopping and terminating an ec2 instance and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Stopping an instance powers it down; EBS volumes persist.\n2. You can start it again later.\n3. Terminating deletes the instance and associated EBS volumes (unless configured otherwise).\n4. Separate the service’s responsibility from the responsibilities of adjacent services, then state the main availability, IAM, networking, or cost consideration when relevant..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an AWS-hosted service, a request or workload can pass through the AWS component described by the answer, and the team can verify the resulting routing, storage, or compute behavior. Stopping an instance powers it down; EBS volumes persist.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an AWS-hosted service, this affects how traffic, compute, storage, identity, or monitoring behaves. Stopping an instance powers it down; EBS volumes persist.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
