    shortDescription: "Compare the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nBucket policies are attached to S3 buckets and define permissions for the bucket and its objects. IAM policies are attached to users/roles and define permissions across all AWS services. Both can be used to grant access.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is s3 bucket policy vs iam policy and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Bucket policies are attached to S3 buckets and define permissions for the bucket and its objects.\n2. IAM policies are attached to users/roles and define permissions across all AWS services.\n3. Both can be used to grant access.\n4. Separate the service’s responsibility from the responsibilities of adjacent services, then state the main availability, IAM, networking, or cost consideration when relevant..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nA production service can use an IAM role for its compute workload so the application obtains temporary permissions rather than embedding long-lived access keys in configuration.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an AWS-hosted service, this affects how traffic, compute, storage, identity, or monitoring behaves. Bucket policies are attached to S3 buckets and define permissions for the bucket and its objects.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
