    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCreate a VPC with a CIDR block. Create two subnets in different AZs: one public (with route to IGW) and one private (with route to NAT Gateway). Associate them with appropriate route tables.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you create a vpc with both public and private subnets, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Create a VPC with a CIDR block.\n2. Create two subnets in different AZs: one public (with route to IGW) and one private (with route to NAT Gateway).\n3. Associate them with appropriate route tables.\n4. Separate the service’s responsibility from the responsibilities of adjacent services, then state the main availability, IAM, networking, or cost consideration when relevant..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the operation in the order you would actually perform it: prerequisite, main action, verification, and the important failure case.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an AWS-hosted service, a request or workload can pass through the AWS component described by the answer, and the team can verify the resulting routing, storage, or compute behavior. Create a VPC with a CIDR block.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an AWS-hosted service, this affects how traffic, compute, storage, identity, or monitoring behaves. Create a VPC with a CIDR block.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
