    slug: "react-vs-react-native",
    shortDescription: "Compare the two frameworks.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nReact targets user interfaces for the web and normally renders to the browser DOM. React Native uses React concepts and JSX but renders to native platform views rather than HTML DOM elements. They share concepts such as components, props, state, and Hooks, but their platform APIs, styling systems, and rendering targets differ.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between react and react native and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. React targets user interfaces for the web and normally renders to the browser DOM.\n2. React Native uses React concepts and JSX but renders to native platform views rather than HTML DOM elements.\n3. They share concepts such as components, props, state, and Hooks, but their platform APIs, styling systems, and rendering targets differ.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a React web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. React targets user interfaces for the web and normally renders to the browser DOM.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a React web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. React targets user interfaces for the web and normally renders to the browser DOM.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
