    slug: "virtual-dom",
    shortDescription: "Explain the concept.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe virtual DOM is a useful mental model for React's in-memory representation of UI. When inputs such as state or props change, React renders a new element tree, reconciles it with the previous tree, and commits the necessary DOM changes. React's goal is not simply to make every DOM update minimal; it also uses scheduling and rendering techniques to keep applications responsive.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the virtual DOM and how does it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. The virtual DOM is a useful mental model for React's in-memory representation of UI.\n2. When inputs such as state or props change, React renders a new element tree, reconciles it with the previous tree, and commits the necessary DOM changes.\n3. React's goal is not simply to make every DOM update minimal; it also uses scheduling and rendering techniques to keep applications responsive.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a React web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. The virtual DOM is a useful mental model for React's in-memory representation of UI.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a React web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. The virtual DOM is a useful mental model for React's in-memory representation of UI.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
