    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nProps are inputs passed into a component by its parent, while state is component-managed data that can change over time. Neither should be mutated directly. Updating state schedules a new render, and new props can cause a component to render again. Props can effectively change when the parent renders with different values, so they are not permanently read-only across the lifetime of a component.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between props and state and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Props are inputs passed into a component by its parent, while state is component-managed data that can change over time.\n2. Neither should be mutated directly.\n3. Updating state schedules a new render, and new props can cause a component to render again.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a React web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. Props are inputs passed into a component by its parent, while state is component-managed data that can change over time.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a React web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Props are inputs passed into a component by its parent, while state is component-managed data that can change over time.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
