    slug: "custom-hook",
    shortDescription: "Define custom hooks.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA custom Hook is a JavaScript function whose name conventionally starts with `use` and that can call other Hooks. It extracts reusable stateful logic, not UI itself. For example, a custom Hook can encapsulate subscription, fetching, or form behavior and return the state and actions a component needs. Each component calling the Hook gets its own Hook state unless the Hook connects to shared external state.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a custom hook accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A custom Hook is a JavaScript function whose name conventionally starts with `use` and that can call other Hooks.\n2. It extracts reusable stateful logic, not UI itself.\n3. For example, a custom Hook can encapsulate subscription, fetching, or form behavior and return the state and actions a component needs.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a React web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. A custom Hook is a JavaScript function whose name conventionally starts with `use` and that can call other Hooks.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a React web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. A custom Hook is a JavaScript function whose name conventionally starts with `use` and that can call other Hooks.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
