    shortDescription: "List the rules.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHooks must be called at the top level of a React function component or custom Hook. Do not call them inside conditions, loops, nested functions, event handlers, or after an early return. React relies on the consistent order of Hook calls across renders to associate Hook state with the correct call.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Hooks must be called at the top level of a React function component or custom Hook.\n2. Do not call them inside conditions, loops, nested functions, event handlers, or after an early return.\n3. React relies on the consistent order of Hook calls across renders to associate Hook state with the correct call.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nKeep the list organized and explain one practical use or limitation for each important item.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a React web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. Hooks must be called at the top level of a React function component or custom Hook.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a React web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Hooks must be called at the top level of a React function component or custom Hook.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
