    slug: "avoid-renders",
    shortDescription: "Explain strategies.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nFirst distinguish unnecessary renders from necessary renders: React may re-render a component even when the resulting DOM changes little, and that is not automatically a performance problem. Profile the application, keep transient state local, avoid unnecessary Effects, split components, and use `memo`, `useMemo`, or `useCallback` when they measurably reduce expensive work.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how can you avoid unnecessary re-renders in react, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. First distinguish unnecessary renders from necessary renders: React may re-render a component even when the resulting DOM changes little, and that is not automatically a performance problem.\n2. Profile the application, keep transient state local, avoid unnecessary Effects, split components, and use `memo`, `useMemo`, or `useCallback` when they measurably reduce expensive work.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a React web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. First distinguish unnecessary renders from necessary renders: React may re-render a component even when the resulting DOM changes little, and that is not automatically a performance problem.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production codebase, how can you avoid unnecessary re-renders in react matters when maintainability, correctness, performance, or user behavior is affected by the choice. The implementation should be tested against the actual path rather than only an isolated example.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
