    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCompound components are a group of related components that cooperate through shared state, often via context. For example, a `Tabs` parent can coordinate `Tabs.List`, `Tabs.Trigger`, and `Tabs.Panel`. The pattern gives consumers a flexible declarative API while keeping coordination logic inside the component family.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Compound components are a group of related components that cooperate through shared state, often via context.\n2. For example, a `Tabs` parent can coordinate `Tabs.List`, `Tabs.Trigger`, and `Tabs.Panel`.\n3. The pattern gives consumers a flexible declarative API while keeping coordination logic inside the component family.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nKeep the list organized and explain one practical use or limitation for each important item.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a React web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. Compound components are a group of related components that cooperate through shared state, often via context.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a React web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Compound components are a group of related components that cooperate through shared state, often via context.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
