    slug: "error-boundaries",
    shortDescription: "Explain error handling.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAn Error Boundary catches errors thrown during rendering, lifecycle methods, and constructors in its descendant tree and renders fallback UI. Traditional Error Boundaries are implemented with class lifecycle methods such as `getDerivedStateFromError` and `componentDidCatch`; there is no built-in function-component Error Boundary API. They do not replace try/catch for event-handler or arbitrary asynchronous errors.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. An Error Boundary catches errors thrown during rendering, lifecycle methods, and constructors in its descendant tree and renders fallback UI.\n2. Traditional Error Boundaries are implemented with class lifecycle methods such as `getDerivedStateFromError` and `componentDidCatch`; there is no built-in function-component Error Boundary API.\n3. They do not replace try/catch for event-handler or arbitrary asynchronous errors.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nName the common items first and give a short purpose for each. Spend most of the time on the distinction most likely to matter in the role.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a React web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. An Error Boundary catches errors thrown during rendering, lifecycle methods, and constructors in its descendant tree and renders fallback UI.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a React web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. An Error Boundary catches errors thrown during rendering, lifecycle methods, and constructors in its descendant tree and renders fallback UI.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
