    question: "What are the pros and cons of using Redux vs Context API?",
    slug: "redux-vs-context-pros-cons",
    shortDescription: "Discuss trade-offs.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nContext is lightweight and built into React, but changing a provider value can update consumers that depend on that context. Redux provides a store architecture with selective subscriptions, middleware, and mature tooling. The right choice depends on state shape and update patterns; it is better to separate contexts or colocate state than to introduce Redux solely because an application is large.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what are the pros and cons of using redux vs context api and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Context is lightweight and built into React, but changing a provider value can update consumers that depend on that context.\n2. Redux provides a store architecture with selective subscriptions, middleware, and mature tooling.\n3. The right choice depends on state shape and update patterns; it is better to separate contexts or colocate state than to introduce Redux solely because an application is large.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a React web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. Context is lightweight and built into React, but changing a provider value can update consumers that depend on that context.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a React web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Context is lightweight and built into React, but changing a provider value can update consumers that depend on that context.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
