    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`BrowserRouter` uses normal URL paths with the browser History API, producing clean URLs such as `/about`; the server must be configured to serve the application entry point for client-side routes on refresh. `HashRouter` stores the route after `#`, which avoids server-side route fallback requirements but produces hash URLs and has different SEO/navigation characteristics.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `browserrouter` and `hashrouter` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `BrowserRouter` uses normal URL paths with the browser History API, producing clean URLs such as `/about`; the server must be configured to serve the application entry point for client-side routes on refresh.\n2. `HashRouter` stores the route after `#`, which avoids server-side route fallback requirements but produces hash URLs and has different SEO/navigation characteristics.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a React web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. `BrowserRouter` uses normal URL paths with the browser History API, producing clean URLs such as `/about`; the server must be configured to serve the application entry point for client-side routes on refresh.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a React web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. `BrowserRouter` uses normal URL paths with the browser History API, producing clean URLs such as `/about`; the server must be configured to serve the application entry point for client-side routes on refresh.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
