    slug: "unit-vs-integration-testing",
    shortDescription: "Compare test types.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA unit test focuses on a small unit of behavior with limited dependencies, while an integration test verifies that multiple pieces work together. In React, a component test that renders a form, interacts with it, and verifies an API-related UI state can be more integration-oriented than a purely isolated unit test. A balanced test suite prioritizes important user-visible behavior.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between unit testing and integration testing in react and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A unit test focuses on a small unit of behavior with limited dependencies, while an integration test verifies that multiple pieces work together.\n2. In React, a component test that renders a form, interacts with it, and verifies an API-related UI state can be more integration-oriented than a purely isolated unit test.\n3. A balanced test suite prioritizes important user-visible behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a React web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. A unit test focuses on a small unit of behavior with limited dependencies, while an integration test verifies that multiple pieces work together.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a React web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. A unit test focuses on a small unit of behavior with limited dependencies, while an integration test verifies that multiple pieces work together.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
