    question: "What is the difference between SSR and CSR (Client-Side Rendering)?",
    slug: "ssr-vs-csr",
    shortDescription: "Compare the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWith CSR, the browser receives an application shell and JavaScript that renders the UI on the client. With SSR, the server generates HTML for the initial request and the client can hydrate it. SSR can improve initial content visibility and crawlability, while CSR can simplify some architectures and reduce server rendering work. Modern frameworks can combine server and client rendering rather than choosing one globally.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between ssr and csr (client-side rendering) and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. With CSR, the browser receives an application shell and JavaScript that renders the UI on the client.\n2. With SSR, the server generates HTML for the initial request and the client can hydrate it.\n3. SSR can improve initial content visibility and crawlability, while CSR can simplify some architectures and reduce server rendering work.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a React web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. With CSR, the browser receives an application shell and JavaScript that renders the UI on the client.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a React web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. With CSR, the browser receives an application shell and JavaScript that renders the UI on the client.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
