    slug: "dev-vs-prod-build",
    shortDescription: "Compare builds.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDevelopment builds prioritize diagnostics, readable errors, and a fast feedback loop, while production builds are optimized for delivery and execution. Production tooling commonly performs minification and dead-code elimination and removes development-only checks. Exact behavior depends on the build tool or framework, so 'React production build' should not be treated as one universal compiler pipeline.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `development` and `production` builds in react and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Development builds prioritize diagnostics, readable errors, and a fast feedback loop, while production builds are optimized for delivery and execution.\n2. Production tooling commonly performs minification and dead-code elimination and removes development-only checks.\n3. Exact behavior depends on the build tool or framework, so 'React production build' should not be treated as one universal compiler pipeline.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a React web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. Development builds prioritize diagnostics, readable errors, and a fast feedback loop, while production builds are optimized for delivery and execution.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a React web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Development builds prioritize diagnostics, readable errors, and a fast feedback loop, while production builds are optimized for delivery and execution.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
