    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA robust modal manages open state, renders an overlay in an appropriate DOM location, traps focus while open, supports Escape where appropriate, restores focus when closed, and exposes correct dialog semantics such as an accessible name. A portal can help avoid clipping and stacking-context problems. Outside-click behavior should be implemented carefully so it does not interfere with clicks inside the dialog.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how would you implement a modal in react, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A robust modal manages open state, renders an overlay in an appropriate DOM location, traps focus while open, supports Escape where appropriate, restores focus when closed, and exposes correct dialog semantics such as an accessible name.\n2. A portal can help avoid clipping and stacking-context problems.\n3. Outside-click behavior should be implemented carefully so it does not interfere with clicks inside the dialog.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the operation in the order you would actually perform it: prerequisite, main action, verification, and the important failure case.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a React web application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. A robust modal manages open state, renders an overlay in an appropriate DOM location, traps focus while open, supports Escape where appropriate, restores focus when closed, and exposes correct dialog semantics such as an accessible name.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a React web application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. A robust modal manages open state, renders an overlay in an appropriate DOM location, traps focus while open, supports Escape where appropriate, restores focus when closed, and exposes correct dialog semantics such as an accessible name.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
