    slug: "redis-overview",
    shortDescription: "Define Redis and list its key features.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRedis (Remote Dictionary Server) is an in-memory data structure store, used as a database, cache, and message broker. It supports various data structures (strings, hashes, lists, sets, sorted sets, streams, etc.). Key features: in-memory performance, persistence (RDB/AOF), replication, Lua scripting, transactions, pub/sub, and high availability with Redis Sentinel and clustering.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain Redis and what are its main features accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Redis (Remote Dictionary Server) is an in-memory data structure store, used as a database, cache, and message broker.\n2. It supports various data structures (strings, hashes, lists, sets, sorted sets, streams, etc.).\n3. Key features: in-memory performance, persistence (RDB/AOF), replication, Lua scripting, transactions, pub/sub, and high availability with Redis Sentinel and clustering.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Redis (Remote Dictionary Server) is an in-memory data structure store, used as a database, cache, and message broker.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Redis (Remote Dictionary Server) is an in-memory data structure store, used as a database, cache, and message broker.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
