    slug: "redis-memory",
    shortDescription: "Explain memory management.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRedis uses a single-threaded event loop and stores data in memory. It uses a memory allocator (jemalloc). You can set `maxmemory` and eviction policies (LRU, LFU, TTL, etc.) to manage memory.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain Redis handle memory management accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Redis uses a single-threaded event loop and stores data in memory.\n2. It uses a memory allocator (jemalloc).\n3. You can set `maxmemory` and eviction policies (LRU, LFU, TTL, etc.) to manage memory.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Redis uses a single-threaded event loop and stores data in memory.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Redis uses a single-threaded event loop and stores data in memory.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
