    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRedis is in-memory and schema-less, with no SQL. It provides high throughput and low latency. SQL databases are disk-based, support complex queries, ACID transactions, and relationships. Redis is often used as a cache or for real-time analytics.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between redis and a traditional relational database and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Redis is in-memory and schema-less, with no SQL.\n2. It provides high throughput and low latency.\n3. SQL databases are disk-based, support complex queries, ACID transactions, and relationships.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Redis is in-memory and schema-less, with no SQL.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Redis is in-memory and schema-less, with no SQL.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
