    shortDescription: "Compare Lists and Streams.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nLists are simple ordered collections; Streams provide persistence, consumer groups, and message acknowledgment. Streams are ideal for message queues; Lists are for simple FIFO/LIFO.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between lists and streams and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Lists are simple ordered collections; Streams provide persistence, consumer groups, and message acknowledgment.\n2. Streams are ideal for message queues; Lists are for simple FIFO/LIFO.\n3. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Lists are simple ordered collections; Streams provide persistence, consumer groups, and message acknowledgment.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Lists are simple ordered collections; Streams provide persistence, consumer groups, and message acknowledgment.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
