    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRDB: compact, faster for large datasets, but data loss on crash. AOF: more durable, but larger files, slower restarts. Use both for best of both worlds.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what are the pros and cons of rdb vs aof and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. RDB: compact, faster for large datasets, but data loss on crash.\n2. AOF: more durable, but larger files, slower restarts.\n3. Use both for best of both worlds.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. RDB: compact, faster for large datasets, but data loss on crash.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a data-backed service, what are the pros and cons of rdb vs aof matters when correctness, access patterns, concurrency, and query or storage cost have to work together. Measure the real workload rather than assuming a design choice is always beneficial.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
