    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nReplica connects to master, sends `SYNC` (or `PSYNC`). Master forks a child to save RDB and sends it to replica. Replica loads the RDB, then master sends incremental updates (replication stream).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain Redis replication accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Replica connects to master, sends `SYNC` (or `PSYNC`).\n2. Master forks a child to save RDB and sends it to replica.\n3. Replica loads the RDB, then master sends incremental updates (replication stream).\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Replica connects to master, sends `SYNC` (or `PSYNC`).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Replica connects to master, sends `SYNC` (or `PSYNC`).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
