    question: "What is an array and what are its time complexities?",
    slug: "array-complexities",
    shortDescription: "Explain arrays and their complexity.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAn array stores elements in an indexed sequence, typically in contiguous memory for a conventional array. Because an index can be translated directly into an element location, random access is O(1). Inserting or deleting in the middle is normally O(n) because elements may need to be shifted. A dynamic array can append in O(1) amortized time, although an individual resize can cost O(n). Key points to remember: - Arrays are fixed-size in many languages (static) or dynamic (like ArrayList). - Access by index is constant time because of direct memory addressing. - Insertion at the end is O(1) if space available, else O(n) for resizing. - Insertion/deletion at arbitrary position requires shifting elements, O(n). - Searching unsorted array is O(n); sorted array can be O(log n) with binary search. For an interview, state the normal/average complexity first, then mention the worst case or.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain an array and what are its time complexities accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. An array stores elements in an indexed sequence, typically in contiguous memory for a conventional array.\n2. Because an index can be translated directly into an element location, random access is O(1).\n3. Inserting or deleting in the middle is normally O(n) because elements may need to be shifted.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. An array stores elements in an indexed sequence, typically in contiguous memory for a conventional array.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. An array stores elements in an indexed sequence, typically in contiguous memory for a conventional array.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
